uired failure items remain. Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity. Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" The phrase \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean that when we double and the resulting granularity is 1? That would require starting at 2 and then doubling to 4, 8... never 1. So perhaps it means that we continue until the granularity becomes 1? But how do we get to 1? Another interpretation: \"singleton granularity\" could mean that when we are trying chunks of size 1 (i.e., single items). Possibly the algorithm reduces granularity after successful removals? Actually \"restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" suggests we increase granularity (coarser means larger chunks) after a successful removal. And \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\" means increase even more. So granularity only increases. So we start at 2, after a success we go to 3, after another success we go to 4? But then after a failure we double from current granularity, so if at 3 and no removal, go to 6, etc. The stopping condition: \"stop at singleton granularity\" is confusing because we never go to 1. Possibly \"singleton granularity\" means when the granularity is 1? That would be if we started at 1? Or maybe it's a misphrasing and it means \"stop when the granularity is greater than or equal to the length of the current list\"? Or stop when we cannot make any more progress? Let's look at the typical chunk reduction algorithm used in test-case minimization: often you start with large chunk sizes and decrease. But here it says start at 2 and increase after success. That seems odd because if you remove a chunk successfully, you'd want to try smaller chunks again? Actually in delta debugging, you start with large chunks and then bisect. Here it says \"start at chunk removal at granularity two\" (small chunks) and then after success go to coarser (larger) chunks. That would mean y